#####
### Title: Tree Coloring for Longest Shared Substring Detection in Colored Trees

**Input format:**  
- `lines`: a character vector containing two sections separated by a single line `"-"`:  
  1. **Adjacency list** describing a rooted tree (`u: v1 v2 v3 ...`).  
  2. **Leaf colors** (`leaf color`).  
- Colors are typically `"red"` and `"blue"` for the Longest Shared Substring problem.

**Objective of the functions:**  
This module implements the **Tree Coloring algorithm** used in *Bioinformatics IV* to identify internal nodes that represent substrings shared between two genomes.

- `parse_tree()`  
  Splits the input into:  
  - `adj`: adjacency list (node → children)  
  - `colors`: initial leaf colors  

- `tree_coloring()`  
  Implements the bottom‑up coloring algorithm:  
  - Unlabeled internal nodes start as `"gray"`  
  - A node becomes **red** if all children are red  
  - A node becomes **blue** if all children are blue  
  - A node becomes **purple** if it has at least one red and one blue descendant  
  - Iterates until no `"gray"` nodes remain  

This is the exact algorithm used to identify **shared substrings** in the suffix tree of two genomes:  
- **purple nodes** correspond to substrings present in both genomes  
- **red/blue nodes** correspond to substrings unique to one genome

**Output format:**  
- A mapping from each node to its final color, printed as:  
  `node color`  
- Nodes are printed in numeric order.

**Coursera module:** Bioinformatics IV — Colored Trees and Longest Shared Substring
#####


# colored trees Longest Shared Substring

# Parse adjacency list and leaf colors
parse_tree <- function(lines) {
  sep_index <- which(lines == "-")
  adj_lines <- lines[1:(sep_index-1)]
  color_lines <- lines[(sep_index+1):length(lines)]
  
  # adjacency list
  adj <- list()
  for (line in adj_lines) {
    parts <- strsplit(line, ":")[[1]]
    node <- trimws(parts[1])
    children <- character(0)
    if (length(parts) > 1 && nchar(trimws(parts[2])) > 0) {
      children <- strsplit(trimws(parts[2]), "\\s+")[[1]]
    }
    adj[[node]] <- children
  }
  
  # initial colors
  colors <- list()
  for (line in color_lines) {
    parts <- strsplit(line, "\\s+")[[1]]
    node <- parts[1]; col <- parts[2]
    colors[[node]] <- col
  }
  
  list(adj=adj, colors=colors)
}

# TreeColoring algorithm
tree_coloring <- function(adj, colors) {
  nodes <- names(adj)
  # initialize gray for unlabeled nodes
  for (n in nodes) {
    if (is.null(colors[[n]])) colors[[n]] <- "gray"
  }
  
  repeat {
    ripe_nodes <- c()
    for (n in nodes) {
      if (colors[[n]] == "gray") {
        children <- adj[[n]]
        if (length(children) > 0 && all(sapply(children, function(c) colors[[c]] != "gray"))) {
          ripe_nodes <- c(ripe_nodes, n)
        }
      }
    }
    if (length(ripe_nodes) == 0) break
    
    for (n in ripe_nodes) {
      child_colors <- unique(unlist(lapply(adj[[n]], function(c) colors[[c]])))
      if (length(child_colors) > 1) {
        colors[[n]] <- "purple"
      } else {
        colors[[n]] <- child_colors[1]
      }
    }
  }
  colors
}

